# Force LF in the working tree on every platform.
#
# Why a catch-all: the conformity pack's promise is a byte-for-byte
# rebuild. The compiler emits the artefacts (manifest.json, the two
# SBOMs, vex, provenance) with LF newlines on every OS, so the only way
# an assessor's regenerated bytes can differ from what ships here is if
# their checkout rewrote the committed LF to CRLF. A checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true does exactly that to any text file not pinned here.
# Pinning every tracked file to eol=lf makes the working tree LF
# regardless of the assessor's autocrlf setting, so the diff stays empty.
#
# Every tracked file in this repo is text (.capa, .json, .md, .toml,
# .sh, .lock, LICENSE, SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH); there are no binaries, so a
# blanket eol=lf is safe. If a binary asset is ever added, give it an
# explicit `*.ext binary` rule below to opt it out.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The reproducibility inputs the rebuild reads directly. The epoch file
# and the generator are pinned LF for defence in depth: even if the
# catch-all above were ever loosened, these must stay LF so the value
# generate.sh reads is a clean decimal and the script body is unchanged.
# (The `tr -d '\r'` in generate.sh strips a stray CR belt-and-braces;
# the compiler's SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH parser actually tolerates a trailing
# CR, so this is hygiene to keep the epoch file clean, not a parser
# requirement.)
conformity/SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH text eol=lf
generate.sh                  text eol=lf
